{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (days 1–10): model is slightly high on average (~+0.2 to +0.6). Apply a small downward bias correction early in the horizon.",
    "Mid-horizon (days 11–22): model tends to over-predict during drawdowns (fails to fall enough). When recent prices are declining, subtract an additional ~0.5–1.5 in this window.",
    "Late-horizon (days 23–30): model systematically underestimates strong rallies/spikes (notably after breakout regimes). When the last 5–10 days show accelerating upside, add a positive ramp that grows with horizon (roughly +1 to +6 by day 30).",
    "Turning points: forecasts appear lagged—too bullish right before/into pullbacks and not bullish enough after upside breaks. Use a momentum-conditioned adjustment: if 5-day momentum < 0, shift the whole path down; if > 0 and rising, shift the back half up.",
    "Overshoot control: after a sharp upside spike in the history, the model often projects excessive mean reversion too soon. Reduce mean-reversion strength (keep prices elevated) for ~1–2 weeks following a breakout."
  ]
}
